1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,360
Here's an experiment you
can do with your lunch.

2
00:00:02,615 --> 00:00:03,765
Take a plain cracker,

3
00:00:03,785 --> 00:00:05,210
with no added sugar.

4
00:00:05,405 --> 00:00:06,680
Chew it, and keep chewing.

5
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:07,660
Don't swallow.

6
00:00:07,845 --> 00:00:09,320
Give it a minute or so,

7
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:11,590
and it starts to
taste a little sweet.

8
00:00:11,610 --> 00:00:13,080
But you didn't add any sugar.

9
00:00:13,175 --> 00:00:15,470
So where's that
sweetness coming from?

10
00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:17,570
A molecule in your saliva.

11
00:00:17,590 --> 00:00:21,180
In a minute, it breaks links that
would otherwise last around a million

12
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:22,000
years.

13
00:00:24,682 --> 00:00:26,772
Crackers are packed with starch.

14
00:00:26,792 --> 00:00:29,947
Zoom right in, and starch
turns out to be a chain.

15
00:00:29,967 --> 00:00:31,412
Hundreds, even thousands,

16
00:00:31,432 --> 00:00:32,972
of glucose molecules,

17
00:00:32,992 --> 00:00:34,612
linked end to end.

18
00:00:34,632 --> 00:00:35,882
Glucose is a sugar,

19
00:00:35,902 --> 00:00:37,677
but locked up in a chain like this,

20
00:00:37,707 --> 00:00:39,087
it doesn't taste sweet.

21
00:00:39,257 --> 00:00:41,037
And those links are tough.

22
00:00:41,057 --> 00:00:43,327
Leave one in water
at room temperature,

23
00:00:43,427 --> 00:00:45,812
and on average it would
last millions of years.

24
00:00:45,965 --> 00:00:47,230
Enter amylase:

25
00:00:47,325 --> 00:00:50,000
an enzyme made in
your salivary glands.

26
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:51,535
It's a carbohydrase:

27
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:53,900
it snips starch into smaller sugars,

28
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:57,765
like maltose, which is just two
glucose units joined together.

29
00:00:57,785 --> 00:00:59,665
And maltose does taste sweet.

30
00:00:59,945 --> 00:01:01,505
That's what an enzyme is:

31
00:01:01,545 --> 00:01:03,455
a biological catalyst.

32
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:06,205
It speeds up a reaction
without being used up,

33
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:08,385
and it comes out unchanged.

34
00:01:08,405 --> 00:01:10,845
Nearly every reaction
in your body has one,

35
00:01:10,875 --> 00:01:13,540
whether it's building things
up or breaking them down.

36
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:18,260
Without enzymes, your metabolism would
be far too slow to keep you alive.

37
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:20,575
Nearly all enzymes are proteins:

38
00:01:20,710 --> 00:01:22,850
long chains of amino acids,

39
00:01:22,955 --> 00:01:24,940
folded into a precise shape.

40
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:26,640
And that shape is everything.

41
00:01:26,828 --> 00:01:29,928
Every enzyme has a small
pocket called the active site.

42
00:01:30,178 --> 00:01:31,577
The molecule it works on,

43
00:01:31,597 --> 00:01:33,707
the substrate, fits inside,

44
00:01:33,767 --> 00:01:36,497
forming an enzyme–substrate complex.

45
00:01:36,517 --> 00:01:39,717
Held there, a bond becomes
much easier to break.

46
00:01:39,737 --> 00:01:41,708
Snap. The products leave,

47
00:01:41,728 --> 00:01:44,433
and the active site is
free for the next one.

48
00:01:44,453 --> 00:01:47,203
The active site is
complementary to the substrate:

49
00:01:47,368 --> 00:01:48,497
not the same shape,

50
00:01:48,517 --> 00:01:49,718
but a perfect fit.

51
00:01:49,918 --> 00:01:51,537
The enzyme is the lock.

52
00:01:51,692 --> 00:01:53,218
The substrate is the key.

53
00:01:53,312 --> 00:01:55,683
That's why enzymes are specific.

54
00:01:55,703 --> 00:01:58,348
Each one only fits its own substrate.

55
00:01:58,368 --> 00:02:01,237
Amylase can't even digest paper.

56
00:02:01,257 --> 00:02:02,717
It's mostly cellulose,

57
00:02:02,737 --> 00:02:04,198
another chain of glucose,

58
00:02:04,263 --> 00:02:06,183
but its links are joined differently,

59
00:02:06,238 --> 00:02:07,613
so it just doesn't fit.

60
00:02:07,793 --> 00:02:09,522
Lock and key is a brilliant model,

61
00:02:09,542 --> 00:02:11,043
but it's a simplification.

62
00:02:11,278 --> 00:02:13,183
Real enzymes aren't rigid.

63
00:02:13,468 --> 00:02:14,748
As the substrate binds,

64
00:02:14,768 --> 00:02:16,942
the active site moulds
itself around it.

65
00:02:17,208 --> 00:02:19,877
That more accurate picture
is called induced fit.

66
00:02:20,173 --> 00:02:22,958
For GCSE and IGCSE,

67
00:02:23,007 --> 00:02:24,757
lock and key is all you need.

68
00:02:25,013 --> 00:02:27,512
Induced fit is an A-level idea.

69
00:02:27,532 --> 00:02:28,562
Just remember:

70
00:02:28,643 --> 00:02:30,992
it's a model, not a photograph.

71
00:02:31,405 --> 00:02:33,155
Which brings us to myth number one:

72
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:34,860
that enzymes get used up.

73
00:02:35,005 --> 00:02:36,000
They don't.

74
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:37,380
After each reaction,

75
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,200
the enzyme is unchanged,

76
00:02:39,225 --> 00:02:40,075
ready to go again,

77
00:02:40,095 --> 00:02:40,910
and again.

78
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:43,315
And some are astonishingly fast.

79
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:46,205
Catalase is found in
almost all your cells.

80
00:02:46,485 --> 00:02:50,075
One molecule of it can break down
hundreds of thousands of molecules of

81
00:02:50,095 --> 00:02:51,620
hydrogen peroxide.

82
00:02:51,855 --> 00:02:52,835
Every second.

83
00:02:53,015 --> 00:02:55,435
Your cells do replace old enzymes,

84
00:02:55,455 --> 00:02:59,060
eventually. But the reaction
itself never uses them up.

85
00:02:59,451 --> 00:03:00,731
Now, temperature.

86
00:03:00,906 --> 00:03:03,246
Molecules are always jiggling around.

87
00:03:03,446 --> 00:03:06,046
Heat them up, and they
gain kinetic energy.

88
00:03:06,066 --> 00:03:07,226
They move faster.

89
00:03:07,486 --> 00:03:10,596
Enzymes and substrates bump
into each other more often,

90
00:03:10,616 --> 00:03:11,911
and with more energy.

91
00:03:12,101 --> 00:03:14,146
So more collisions are successful,

92
00:03:14,166 --> 00:03:16,911
with the substrate landing
in the active site.

93
00:03:16,931 --> 00:03:19,416
More enzyme–substrate complexes form,

94
00:03:19,436 --> 00:03:20,896
and the rate goes up.

95
00:03:20,916 --> 00:03:23,441
Up to a point, warmer means faster.

96
00:03:23,781 --> 00:03:26,651
The temperature where the rate
peaks is called the optimum.

97
00:03:26,961 --> 00:03:28,691
For many enzymes in your body,

98
00:03:28,711 --> 00:03:30,546
it's around 37 degrees:

99
00:03:30,686 --> 00:03:31,846
body temperature.

100
00:03:32,145 --> 00:03:33,880
So what if we keep
turning up the heat?

101
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:34,985
Fifty degrees.

102
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:36,745
Sixty. Seventy.

103
00:03:36,805 --> 00:03:38,880
Does it just keep getting faster?

104
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:42,040
Pause the video, and
make your prediction.

105
00:03:45,005 --> 00:03:46,610
Here's what actually happens.

106
00:03:46,785 --> 00:03:48,980
The rate crashes, and
it doesn't recover.

107
00:03:49,170 --> 00:03:52,785
An enzyme's shape is held
together by lots of weak bonds.

108
00:03:52,805 --> 00:03:57,215
Too much heat, and it vibrates so
violently that those bonds break.

109
00:03:57,235 --> 00:03:58,595
The chain unravels,

110
00:03:58,615 --> 00:04:00,530
the active site loses its shape,

111
00:04:00,685 --> 00:04:02,735
and the substrate no longer fits.

112
00:04:02,970 --> 00:04:05,180
The enzyme has been denatured.

113
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:09,610
It's the same kind of change that
turns egg white solid in a hot pan.

114
00:04:09,630 --> 00:04:11,035
You can't uncook an egg,

115
00:04:11,150 --> 00:04:13,840
and cooling usually won't
fix the enzyme either.

116
00:04:14,035 --> 00:04:15,320
Cold is different.

117
00:04:15,525 --> 00:04:17,675
Molecules have less kinetic energy,

118
00:04:17,695 --> 00:04:19,285
so there are fewer collisions.

119
00:04:19,530 --> 00:04:21,515
But the enzyme isn't denatured.

120
00:04:21,705 --> 00:04:23,450
Warm it up, and it recovers.

121
00:04:24,310 --> 00:04:25,740
And that's myth number two:

122
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:27,470
that heat "kills" enzymes.

123
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,270
Enzymes were never alive.

124
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,225
An enzyme is a molecule,

125
00:04:31,245 --> 00:04:32,260
a very clever one,

126
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:33,405
but it can't die.

127
00:04:33,655 --> 00:04:35,040
It's denatured.

128
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:36,925
Its active site changes shape,

129
00:04:36,945 --> 00:04:38,800
so the substrate no longer fits,

130
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:41,885
and no enzyme–substrate
complex can form.

131
00:04:41,905 --> 00:04:44,645
In an exam, that's the
answer that gets the marks.

132
00:04:44,916 --> 00:04:48,191
pH, how acidic or
alkaline something is,

133
00:04:48,306 --> 00:04:50,651
can denature enzymes too.

134
00:04:50,671 --> 00:04:52,026
Too far either way,

135
00:04:52,101 --> 00:04:55,541
and the bonds holding the active
site in shape are disrupted.

136
00:04:55,561 --> 00:04:58,171
So every enzyme has an optimum pH.

137
00:04:58,436 --> 00:04:59,661
Amylase, in your mouth,

138
00:04:59,681 --> 00:05:01,341
works best close to neutral,

139
00:05:01,361 --> 00:05:03,176
around pH 7.

140
00:05:03,196 --> 00:05:05,181
Your stomach is strongly acidic,

141
00:05:05,201 --> 00:05:07,351
often around pH 2.

142
00:05:07,371 --> 00:05:10,641
And pepsin, a protease that
digests protein there,

143
00:05:10,661 --> 00:05:12,591
works best right around that.

144
00:05:12,611 --> 00:05:13,661
Different enzymes,

145
00:05:13,681 --> 00:05:14,971
tuned to different places.

146
00:05:15,578 --> 00:05:17,088
So, back to that cracker.

147
00:05:17,308 --> 00:05:19,728
As you chew, amylase
meets the starch.

148
00:05:19,968 --> 00:05:21,883
The chains slot into its active site,

149
00:05:21,903 --> 00:05:23,423
and it snips them into maltose,

150
00:05:23,443 --> 00:05:25,568
over and over, without being used up.

151
00:05:25,588 --> 00:05:27,583
Your mouth is about 37 degrees,

152
00:05:27,603 --> 00:05:29,038
and close to neutral.

153
00:05:29,058 --> 00:05:29,858
Just right.

154
00:05:30,073 --> 00:05:32,898
Links that would last a million
years or more on their own?

155
00:05:33,023 --> 00:05:34,523
Broken while you chew.

156
00:05:34,543 --> 00:05:36,028
That sweet taste is you,

157
00:05:36,048 --> 00:05:38,088
tasting enzymes at work.

158
00:05:38,108 --> 00:05:39,578
It all comes down to shape.

159
00:05:39,803 --> 00:05:41,743
And that's how enzymes actually work.
